Title:
Defender Internals – AI Assisted EDR Introspection

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post reverse engineers Microsoft Defender’s behavior monitoring pipeline by correlating live process-injection activity with Defender’s ETW-driven reactions and then tracing the resulting call stacks into `mpengine.dll`. It shows that during a typical Alloc/Write/Protect/CreateRemoteThread injection sequence, Defender often responds by opening and memory-scanning the *injector* process (enumerating modules via PEB/LDR and reading the injector’s PE image), rather than immediately scanning the target process. The author reconstructs an internal architecture model: ETW events are decoded into a `BmBehaviorPayload`, wrapped as `INotification` objects, queued per `ProcessContext`, and processed by worker threads that may schedule process-memory scans. The write-up details the decision path from Threat-Intelligence ETW (e.g., `KERNEL_THREATINT_TASK_PROTECTVM`) into a normalized internal event (`BM_Etw_CodeInjection/protectvm`), taint propagation, “friendly” suppression checks, and finally the process-memory scan pipeline (`OpenProcess`, `VirtualQueryEx`, `ReadProcessMemory`). It also documents Defender’s verdict caching/correlation layer (`BM_MetaStore`) and its SQLite-backed database (`mpenginedb.db`). Useful for red teamers and security researchers building a mental model of Defender’s injection detections and for blue teamers validating what telemetry/behavior actually triggers scans.

Technical Focus:
- ETW Threat Intelligence telemetry correlation (ALLOCVM/PROTECTVM/READVM, OpenProcess auditing)
- Defender Behavior Monitoring internals (`BmBehaviorPayload`, `INotification`, per-process queues)
- Process injection detection flow and taint propagation (source/target process graph)
- Process memory scanning implementation details (`OpenProcess`, `VirtualQueryEx`, `ReadProcessMemory`, PE header/section reads)
- Verdict caching and suppression logic (`BM_MetaStore`, “friendly” checks)
- Defender local persistence via SQLite (`mpenginedb.db`) and schema artifacts

Use Cases:
- Reproducing and timing Defender reactions to common injection primitives (Alloc/Write/Protect/CreateRemoteThread)
- Mapping ETW events to Defender internal detection stages for research and tooling
- Identifying which injector behaviors trigger source-process memory scans vs. correlation-only checks
- Building more accurate detection engineering hypotheses around Defender’s BM pipeline and caching
- Offline analysis of Defender’s local MetaStore/engine DB for research and forensics

Keywords:
Microsoft Defender, MsMpEng.exe, mpengine.dll, ETW, Microsoft-Windows-Threat-Intelligence, KERNEL_THREATINT_TASK_PROTECTVM, KERNEL_THREATINT_TASK_ALLOCVM, KERNEL_THREATINT_TASK_READVM, process injection, VirtualAllocEx, WriteProcessMemory, VirtualProtectEx, CreateRemoteThread, OpenProcess, VirtualQueryEx, ReadProcessMemory, PEB, PEB_LDR_DATA, LDR_DATA_TABLE_ENTRY, Behavior Monitoring, INotification, ProcessContext, BM_MetaStore, taint propagation, friendly suppression, SQLite, mpenginedb.db, Ghidra, Ghidra-MCP, GPT-assisted reversing